==Status Report: 2026-10-11 22:25 PDT== (research — no implementation)
Answers to your three questions, as concretely as I can be honest about.

(0) "Should we be smashing it to ASCII? Are we losing pronunciation info?"

Short answer: the ASCII fold itself is not where we lose pronunciation. It is the
*delivery path* (plain `TextToSpeech.speak(String)`) that has no way to act on
pronunciation, so the Unicode we drop (é, č, å, ø, ç, …) would not actually help
the named engines through that call even if we kept it.

Quantification: this is data, not code, so retaining Unicode would NOT change APK
size at all. The transliterate stage is a runtime string transform over already-
Latin-dominant English Wikipedia prose; the marginal non-ASCII per article is a
few KB of foreign names/terms. So "keeping Unicode grows the APK" is not a real
cost — my earlier size concern was wrong in framing. The real trade-off is:
  • Keep Unicode → we hold mora/letter-accent info, but the standard
    `engine.speak(text)` path sends a bare string with no per-token locale/phoneme
    params, so a grapheme like "é" reaches engines that do their own G2P and get
    folded to "e" by them anyway (Kokoro/Pocket tokenize to a fixed phoneme-for-
    letter vocabulary). Net gain: near zero.
  • So the decision is not "ASCII vs Unicode" but "does the engine accept IPA via
    SSML" — which is your question (1). If the answer is "only a narrow set", then
    neither ASCII nor Unicode helps, and the only engine-agnostic output is an
    ASCII *pronunciation respelling* (e.g. IPA → "vair-SIGH").

Recommendation: keep the ASCII fold (it is what makes all our regex rules work on
one clean alphabet), and spend the effort on stage 3's output encoding, not on
retaining Unicode. Rejected Unicode-retention again, but for the *correct* reason
this time (delivery path, not size).

(1) Exactly which engines honor SSML / inline IPA? (what is that "narrow set")

The platform surface is the issue. Android's `TextToSpeech` has NO first-class
SSML. `speak(text, queueMode, params, utteranceId)` passes `text` to the engine
verbatim; the only standardised knobs are a handful of `TextToSpeech.Engine.KEY_*`
bundle extras (pitch/speech rate/volume/pan), none of which is "phoneme" or
"IPA". There is also `synthesizeToFile` (some engines support a `KEY_PARAM_...`
for output format, but again nothing standard for per-word phonemes).

So "SSML/IPA via the standard API" is essentially undocumented, engine-specific
territory:
  • Some engines parse a subset of SSML (`<phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="…">`, or
    `<say-as>`) *inside the passed string* — notably eSpeak (honors IPA phonemes
    and its own `[[…]]` syntax) and a few Linux-derived front-ends. Google's
    network engine does NOT reliably honor inline IPA in `speak()`; it mostly
    ignores `<phoneme>` markup in the text argument.
  • Kokoro and Pocket TTS (local neural engines) expose their own text/SSML
    handling, but their *Android* ports do not document a stable IPA-in-speak
    contract through the platform `TextToSpeech` shim. Whether they honor
    `<phoneme>` depends on the port's shim, not the model.
  • The only portably-correct IPC for "synthesize this IPA to audio" is
    `synthesizeToFile`/`addSpeech` with an engine that supports it — still
    engine-specific and not guaranteed.

Conclusion: inline IPA/SSML through `speak()` is a red herring for this app's
current plain-text model; it would work for, at best, a narrow, undocumented,
engine-specific set and break the moment the engine changes. That is exactly the
"narrow set" I flagged. The robust, engine-agnostic stage-3 output is a plain-ASCII
pronunciation respelling (pushed through the existing Normalizer pipeline), which
works on every engine including the two you care about. If we ever want true IPA
for a specific engine later, it is an opt-in per-engine knob, not v1.

(2) Lazy inline vs. upfront full-article normalization (your post-load idea)

Your instinct is right and it is *not* feature creep — it is the correct
architecture given how SpeechController already works (it enqueues the whole
article up front in one pass). Proposed shape:

  • On article load, run the full normalization (and stage-1 foreign-word
    detection) over the *entire article* once, in the background, as the article
    is displayed. The normalizer is pure string transform (fast; no network).
  • Stage-2 (IPA lookup for detected non-English words) becomes ONE batched
    request for all unknown words, fired right after load, not per paragraph.
    This directly removes the per-paragraph Wikimedia-weight concern.
  • Cache the normalized text per article: transiently in memory (or a small
    temp store) for un-bookmarked articles, and inside the bookmark store (ticket
    11) for bookmarked ones — so re-listening never re-fetches or re-normalizes.

This trades a small one-time delay on first open for zero per-paragraph latency
and batched (not burst) API usage. Recommend we adopt it as the stage-2/3
architecture. Still research — no code written.

